<article class="ck-case">
  <header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Carrabba’s Italian Grill / Bloomin’ Brands</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">Carrabba’s</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">40% faster access</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Content strategy &amp; UX architecture</p><h1>From table<br />to kitchen.</h1></div><div><p class="lede">Carrabba’s Tableside brought recipes and Italian cooking techniques into a branded editorial platform. Through Voce Communications and Porter Novelli, I led UX strategy, information architecture, visual design, and the WordPress build as Senior UX Strategist.</p><dl><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Senior UX Strategist</dd></div><div><dt>Result</dt><dd>40% faster time-to-recipe</dd></div></dl></div></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "The brief"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Content architecture"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Reading experience"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Brand details"}, {"href": "#case-section-5", "label": "Outcome"}]} />
  <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/carrabbas_hero.webp" alt="Carrabba’s restaurant kitchen" loading="eager" /></figure>
<section class="ck-detail" aria-labelledby="ck-brief"><div><p class="ck-detail__label">The brief</p><h2 id="ck-brief">Make the content worth coming back for.</h2></div><div class="ck-detail__copy"><p>Carrabba’s wanted to reach food enthusiasts who might not already be restaurant guests. The platform’s editorial anchor, Secrets from Carrabba’s Kitchen, offered a way to introduce the brand through useful cooking knowledge.</p><p>The UX challenge was balancing that brand presence with the reader’s immediate purpose. I treated the content as a culinary resource, with recipes, techniques, and chef expertise carrying the relationship. The publishing workflow also needed guidance so new articles would support the same experience.</p></div></section>
  <section class="opening" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / Reframing the job</p><h2>Teach the technique.<br />Earn the return visit.</h2></div><div><p>I ran moderated usability sessions with 12 home cooks across three skill levels. Experienced cooks tended to look for techniques and ingredients, while novices looked for a dish or outcome they could achieve. The navigation needed to support both approaches.</p><p>I also audited eight food content platforms and three restaurant blogs. A useful distinction emerged between browsing for inspiration and following a recipe. Discovery needed informative previews and several ways into the content. Cooking needed clear instructions and fewer distractions.</p></div></section>
  <section class="blueprint" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas01.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen wireframes" loading="lazy" /></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / Content architecture</p><h2>Many ways in.<br />One clear way through.</h2><p>I organized content around techniques and ingredients, with Popular, Recent, and topical filters providing different routes into the same library. Kitchen Secrets gave the editorial content a recognizable home. These paths let readers browse by interest without requiring them to know a recipe’s exact name.</p><p>Card previews supplied enough context to judge relevance before opening an article. Titles such as “How to Boil the Perfect Pot of Pasta” described the skill a reader could learn. Chef-led content connected that usefulness to Carrabba’s identity.</p><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas02.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen mobile recipe discovery" loading="lazy" /></figure></div></section>
  <figure class="mobile-field"><img src="/images/carrabbas03.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen across mobile screens" loading="lazy" /></figure>
  <section class="reading" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Made for the kitchen</p><h2>Read, cook,<br />look back.</h2><p>I designed recipe pages around the practical limits of using a phone in a kitchen: one hand may be occupied, the screen may be at arm’s length, and attention shifts between the food and the instructions. A vertical layout, large touch targets, and clear ingredient and instruction hierarchy supported that use.</p><p>The browsing interface could offer several choices at once. Once a reader opened a recipe, the layout reduced competing elements and kept the steps easy to return to after an interruption.</p></div><div class="reading__images"><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas04.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen mobile interface collection" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas05.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen chef portrait" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas06.webp" alt="Carrabba’s pasta recipe" loading="lazy" /></figure></div></section>
  <section class="warmth" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas07.webp" alt="Carrabba’s family food moment" loading="lazy" /></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / Brand in the details</p><h2>Warmth without clutter.</h2><p>Food photography led the visual hierarchy, with in-process cooking images supporting the platform’s focus on technique. Carrabba’s Tableside serif carried the brand at title scale, paired with a clean sans-serif for readable body text on mobile.</p><p>I coordinated with the agency building Carrabba’s main website to establish a shared visual language. The editorial platform needed to feel related to the restaurant site while serving a different task: helping someone learn and cook.</p></div></section>
<section class="ck-detail" aria-labelledby="ck-editorial"><div><p class="ck-detail__label">Editorial system &amp; handoff</p><h2 id="ck-editorial">Give the content team the reasoning behind the layouts.</h2></div><div class="ck-detail__copy"><p>I added editorial guidelines to the scope after identifying how much the experience depended on future content. The guidance covered action-oriented titles, content length, image selection, and category tagging. These decisions connected the writing and publishing process to the information architecture.</p><p>I led the WordPress build alongside the UX and visual design work. The handoff gave editors a framework for adding articles consistently, including how to label a story and select images that helped explain a technique.</p></div></section>
  <section class="outcome" id="case-section-5" tabindex="-1"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / Outcome</p><h2>A system the<br />content team could keep cooking with.</h2><div class="stats"><div><b>40%</b><span>faster time-to-recipe</span></div><div><b>28%</b><span>more mobile session depth</span></div><div><b>3 weeks</b><span>to independent publishing</span></div></div><p class="outcome__context">The original project account reports 40% faster access to content in task-timed usability sessions with 12 participants, a 28% increase in mobile session depth after launch, and independent publishing within three weeks. These describe different measures of the project’s outcome; they do not establish that any single design change caused the engagement increase.</p></div><figure><img src="/images/carrabbas08.webp" alt="Carrabba’s Kitchen responsive publishing system" loading="lazy" /></figure></section>
<section class="ck-detail" aria-labelledby="ck-reflection"><div><p class="ck-detail__label">Reflection</p><h2 id="ck-reflection">Observe the experience in a working kitchen.</h2></div><div class="ck-detail__copy"><p>I would extend the post-launch research with direct observation of people using the platform while cooking. Usability sessions helped test the navigation, but longer-term use could show where readers lose their place, return to an ingredient list, or stop following a recipe.</p><p>That feedback would help prioritize changes to the reading experience and editorial guidance. Analytics could identify where to look; observing the task would help explain what needed to change.</p></div></section>
</article>
